e":10,"criticalRenown":18,"controversy":8,"llmAffinity":82,"affinityResidual":12.27,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.12,"criticalRenown":0.12,"controversy":0.12,"llmAffinity":0.88},"tags":{"craft":"voice","evidence":"personal-experience","stance":"moral-seriousness"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"summary":"\"Arnold Thomas Fanning had his first experience of depression during adolescence, following the death of his mother. Some ten years later, an up-and-coming playwright, he was overcome by mania and delusions. Thus began a terrible period in which he was often suicidal, increasingly disconnected from family and friends, sometimes in trouble with the law, and homeless in London.Drawing on his own memories, the recollections of people who knew him when he was at his worst, and medical and police records, Arnold Thomas Fanning has produced a beautifully written, devastatingly intense account of madness - and recovery, to the point where he has not had any serious illness for over a decade and has become an acclaimed playwright. In a remarkably vivid present-tense narrative, Fanning manages to convey the consciousness of a person living with mania, psychosis and severe depression. Very few people have gone through what Arnold Thomas Fanning went through and emerged alive, well, and capable of telling the tale with such skill and insight.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Depression, mental","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-mind-on-fire-a-memoir-of-madness-and-recovery-arnold-thomas-fanning"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Psychoses","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-mind-on-fire-a-memoir-of-madness-and-recovery-arnold-thomas-fanning"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Mental illness, treatment","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-mind-on-fire-a-memoir-of-madness-and-recovery-arnold-thomas-fanning"},{"source":"open_library","